Q1. What is access control and why is it central to application security?
Ans:
Access control decides who can do what on which resource.
Authentication answers who you are.
Access control enforces what you’re allowed to do.

-> What’s really happening:
For every request, the system must evaluate:
	• identity (user / service)
	• action (read, write, delete)
	• resource (object, record, API)
	• context (tenant, state, business rules)
This decision must happen before the action executes.

-> Where systems fail:
	• Access checks done only at:
		○ UI
		○ API gateway
	• Backend trusts incoming identifiers blindly

Q2. What is IDOR and why is it so common?
Ans:
IDOR happens when users can access other users’ data by changing an identifier.

-> What’s really happening:
	• Client sends a resource ID (orderId, userId)
	• Backend:
		○ fetches the object
		○ does not verify ownership
	• Any authenticated user can enumerate data

-> Why it’s so common:
	• Developers check authentication
	• They forget object-level authorization
	• IDs look random, so they “feel safe”

-> Impact:
	• Data leakage
	• Account takeover paths
	• Compliance violations

Q3. Why are role-based checks alone insufficient for access control?
Ans:
Having the right role doesn’t mean you own the data.

-> What’s really happening:
	• Two users:
		○ same role
		○ different resources
	• Role check passes
	• Ownership check is missing
Real-world example
	• Both users are “customers”
	• One accesses another customer’s order

-> Correct approach:
Access = role AND object relationship

Q4. Where should access control be enforced in a system?
Ans:
Access control must be enforced close to the data.

-> What’s really happening:
	• Frontend:
		○ improves UX
		○ cannot be trusted
	• API gateway:
		○ enforces coarse rules
		○ lacks business context
	• Backend service:
		○ knows:
			- ownership
			- tenant
			- state
			- business rules

-> Correct design:
	• Backend = final authority
	• Gateway = defense in depth

Q5. How do you prevent IDOR in practice?
Ans:
Never trust client-supplied identifiers.

-> What’s really happening:
For every request:
	1. Identify the caller
	2. Fetch the resource securely
	3. Verify:
		○ ownership or
		○ explicit permission
	4. Only then perform the action

-> Good patterns:
	• Query by:
		○ resource ID + user ID
	• Centralized authorization logic
	• Deny by default

-> Bad patterns:
	• “If ID exists, return it”
	• Security through obscurity (UUIDs)

Q6. How does IDOR appear in APIs even when IDs are not exposed directly?
Ans:
Hiding IDs does not prevent IDOR.

-> What’s really happening:
	• APIs often use:
		○ indirect references
		○ encoded IDs
		○ UUIDs
	• Backend still:
		○ resolves the reference
		○ fetches the object
		○ skips ownership check

-> Why this fails:
	• Attackers don’t need to guess IDs
	• They reuse:
		○ previously seen references
		○ references from logs, emails, URLs

-> Correct mindset:
Authorization, not obscurity, prevents IDOR.

Q7. How does IDOR manifest in multi-tenant systems?
Ans:
IDOR becomes cross-tenant data leakage.

-> What’s really happening:
	• Requests include:
		○ resource ID
		○ sometimes tenant ID
	• Backend:
		○ trusts tenant ID from client
		○ does not enforce tenant scoping
Common failure
	• Querying by:
		○ resource ID only
	• Not binding:
		○ user → tenant → resource

-> Correct pattern:
Every access check must enforce:
	• tenant isolation
	• object ownership

Q8. What is indirect object reference and how is it different from IDOR?
Ans: 
Indirect references change how objects are referenced, not how access is controlled.

-> What’s really happening
	• Direct reference:
		○ /orders/123
	• Indirect reference:
		○ /orders/abcxyz
	• Backend maps:
		○ abcxyz → 123
Key mistake
	• Developers assume indirection = security
	• Authorization checks are still missing
Important truth
Indirect references do not replace access control.

Q9. How should access control be tested effectively?
Ans:
You test access control by breaking assumptions, not following happy paths.

-> What’s really happening
Effective testing includes:
	• horizontal access attempts (peer data)
	• vertical access attempts (privilege escalation)
	• cross-tenant access
What to look for
	• Missing checks
	• Inconsistent enforcement
	• Partial protections
Why automated tests miss this
	• Tools don’t understand business context
	• Human reasoning is required

Q10. How do you design access control so it scales and stays correct?
Ans:
Access control should be centralized, explicit, and default-deny.

-> What’s really happening:
Good systems:
	• define access rules clearly
	• enforce them consistently
	• avoid scattered logic
Design principles
	• One source of truth for authorization
	• Explicit permission checks
	• Fail closed, not open
Red flags
	• Copy-paste access checks
	• “We’ll add it later”
	• UI-based enforcement

🔥11. “Can IDOR exist in POST or PATCH requests?”
Example
POST /update-email
{
   "userId": 123,
   "email": "attacker@gmail.com"
}

Backend:
UPDATE users SET email = ? WHERE id = ?

If attacker changes:
"userId": 456
They update someone else’s email.
That is IDOR.

Why This Is Subtle
Because developer thinks:
“It’s not data retrieval, it’s update.”
But authorization applies to:
	• Read
	• Write
	• Delete
	• Modify
	• Upload
	• Download
Any operation on object must verify ownership.

IDOR is not limited to read operations; any object-level action without contextual authorization is vulnerable.

🔥12.  Multi-Tenant Saa→ Cross-Tenant Data Breachch
🏗️ Whiteboard Architecture
User → Web App → Shared Database
Two tenants:
	• Tenant A
	• Tenant B
Table:
invoices
----------------
id
tenant_id
amount

User from Tenant A logs in.
🔎 Vulnerable Endpoint
GET /api/invoices/9001
Backend code:
SELECT * FROM invoices WHERE id = 9001;

Notice what is missing?
No tenant check.

🧨 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Attacker logs in normally (Tenant A).
Step 2 – They access:
/api/invoices/9001
Step 3 – That invoice belongs to Tenant B.
Step 4 – Backend returns it because it only checks:
	• Is user authenticated? ✅
	• Does invoice exist? ✅
It does NOT check:
	• Does invoice belong to user’s tenant? ❌
Data breach.

💡 Why This Happens
Developers think:
“User is logged in → allowed.”
They forget contextual authorization:
“Allowed to access THIS object?”

🛡️ Fix (Architecture Level)
Query must always enforce tenant boundary:
SELECT * FROM invoices 
WHERE id = ? AND tenant_id = current_user.tenant_id;
Better:
	• Database row-level security.
	• Central authorization middleware.

🔥 13. Horizontal Privilege Escalatio→ Profile Modificationon
🏗️ Architecture
User → REST API → Users Table
Endpoint:
PUT /api/users/{userId}

🔎 Vulnerable Code
UPDATE users SET email = ? WHERE id = ?;

🧨 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Attacker logs in as user 101.
Step 2 – They intercept request:
PUT /api/users/101

Step 3 – Change it to:
PUT /api/users/102
Step 4 – Backend updates user 102’s email.
No ownership check.

💡 Why This Happens
Developers assume:
“If you’re authenticated, you can update your profile.”
But they trust the user-supplied userId.
They should derive userId from session instead.

🛡️ Fix
Never trust client-supplied object ID for self operations.
Instead of:
PUT /api/users/{id}
Use:
PUT /api/me
And derive ID from session.

Or enforce:
UPDATE users 
SET email = ? 
WHERE id = ? AND id = current_user.id;

🔥 14 File Download System → Sensitive Document Leak
🏗️ Architecture
User → App → File Storage (S3 or local)
Download endpoint:
GET /download?fileId=48291
Database:
files
----------------
id
owner_id
path

🔎 Vulnerable Code
SELECT path FROM files WHERE id = ?;
No ownership check.

🧨 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Attacker downloads their own file.
Step 2 – They change:
fileId=48291 → 48292
Step 3 – That file belongs to another user.
Step 4 – App fetches and streams file.
Sensitive document leaked.

💡 Why This Happens
Object identity ≠ authorization.

🛡️ Fix
SELECT path FROM files 
WHERE id = ? AND owner_id = current_user.id;
Even better:
	• Generate short-lived signed URLs only after authorization.

🔥 15. Admin Role Upgrade → Vertical Privilege Escalationon
Now we go deeper.
🏗️ Architecture
User → API → Role Table
Endpoint:
PATCH /api/users/{id}/role

🔎 Vulnerable Code
UPDATE users SET role = ? WHERE id = ?;
Backend checks:
	• User is authenticated
	• User has “manager” role
But does NOT check:
	• Can manager modify THIS user?

🧨 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Attacker is “manager”.
Step 2 – They modify:
PATCH /api/users/attackerId
{
  "role": "admin"
}
Step 3 – Backend allows because:
	• Manager role exists
	• Update query succeeds
Now attacker becomes admin.

💡 Why This Happens
Authorization is role-based only.
Not object-sensitive.
Vertical privilege escalation via IDOR.

🛡️ Fix
Authorization must enforce:
	• Only admins can change roles
	• Or cannot modify own role
	• Or cannot assign higher privilege than self
Access control must be contextual and hierarchical.

🔥 16. GraphQL API → Hidden Object Exposure
Modern systems use GraphQL.
🏗️ Architecture
User → GraphQL → Database
Query:
query {
  order(id: "9001") {
    id
    amount
    userId
  }
}

🔎 Vulnerable Resolver
Resolver only checks:
	• User is authenticated
Does NOT check:
	• order.userId == current_user.id

🧨 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Attacker runs query for their own order.
Step 2 – They modify:
order(id: "9002")
Step 3 – Backend returns someone else's order.

GraphQL makes this worse because:
	• Single endpoint
	• Many object types
	• Deep nested queries
Developers often forget object-level checks inside resolvers.

💡 Why This Happens
GraphQL encourages field-level development.
Authorization logic gets scattered or forgotten.

🛡️ Fix
Each resolver must enforce:
if (order.userId != currentUser.id) deny();
Or use centralized policy engine.

🧠 The Pattern Behind All 5
Every IDOR chain follows this structure:
1. User controls object identifier
2. Backend retrieves object by ID
3. Backend fails to verify ownership or permission
4. Sensitive data exposed or modified

“What is IDOR at architecture level?”
IDOR is an object-level authorization failure where the system enforces authentication but does not verify whether the authenticated user has access rights to the specific object referenced.

Summary :
Auth ≠ Access
Roles ≠ Ownership
IDs ≠ Permission
Backend ≠ Trustless
Obscurity ≠ Security
Tenant ≠ User
Reference ≠ Permission
Testing breaks assumptions
Default deny wins


